{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779130562,"nonce":"McGTf2CF8EhAhX/5DQdoI2Rltpg=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABComH4g","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/129","origin":"github"},"title":"Purge router-as-NTP refs from chrony hosts + op-router DHCP option 42; verify public-pool fallback","message":"## Context\n\nThe 2026-05-18 network audit and follow-up confirmed:\n\n1. The 04-10 plan to run ISC ntpd on both RUTX11 routers was **wiped by a firmware update on/around 2026-04-21** (`Tue Apr 21 22:07:15 2026 system.notice Firmware update to RUTX_R_00.07.22.1 is now available` in boat-router logread). The custom UCI config survived; the `ntp` package did not. Neither router currently serves NTP.\n2. Chrony configs on **all three Linux hosts** still reference the routers as NTP servers. They show `Reach=0` today (routers aren't responding), so chrony quietly falls back to the public Ubuntu pools already configured in `/etc/chrony/chrony.conf`. **Time is correct right now** — but the refs are a footgun: if `untpd` is ever started, chrony would lock onto its stratum-1-lie over the real public pools. That was the exact failure mode the original ISC ntpd switch was meant to avoid.\n3. Op router still advertises itself via **DHCP option 42** (`list dhcp_option '42,192.168.13.1'`), telling DHCP clients to use it for NTP — even though it doesn't actually serve NTP.\n\nParent context: rolker/unh_echoboats_project11#128.\n\n## Scope\n\nThree pieces, one PR or sibling PRs:\n\n### A. Chrony cleanup on three Linux hosts\n\nEdit `/etc/chrony/sources.d/project11.sources` on **gabby**, **salmon**, and **deadpool** to remove router NTP references. Keep TM2000B references (still the right canonical source when reachable). Public-pool fallback already present in `/etc/chrony/chrony.conf` (Ubuntu defaults) — no changes needed there.\n\n**Current state (audit excerpts):**\n\n- `gabby:/etc/chrony/sources.d/project11.sources` — has `time.bizzy.p11.lan iburst prefer` ✅, plus `router.bizzy.p11.lan` and `router.op.p11.lan` (to remove).\n- `salmon:/etc/chrony/sources.d/project11.sources` — has TM2000B (LAN + VPN), plus `router.op.p11.lan`, `router.bizzy.p11.lan`, `router.vpn.bizzy.p11.lan` (to remove). Has `time.unh.edu` and `gabby.bizzy.p11.lan noselect` (keep).\n- `deadpool:/etc/chrony/sources.d/project11.sources` — similar to salmon's pattern (to clean up).\n\n**Proposed gabby file:**\n```\n# BizzyBoat TM2000B GPS NTP (stratum 1, local LAN — preferred when reachable)\nserver time.bizzy.p11.lan iburst prefer\n\n# Public internet pools provided by /etc/chrony/chrony.conf as last-resort fallback.\n# Routers are not currently NTP servers (firmware-wiped ISC ntpd, untpd disabled);\n# see rolker/unh_echoboats_project11#128.\n```\n\n**Proposed salmon/deadpool file** (similar shape, with VPN path + UNH source):\n```\nserver time.bizzy.p11.lan iburst prefer\nserver time.vpn.bizzy.p11.lan iburst        # lower-priority via VPN\nserver time.unh.edu iburst                   # UNH-internal fallback\n# Routers excluded — see #128.\n```\n\n**Track configs in repo:** add to `ccomjhc_project11/configuration/\u003chost\u003e/chrony/project11.sources` so the canonical version lives in git (paralleling the existing `configuration/ben/` etc. pattern). Sub-task PR will land there.\n\nAfter file push: `sudo systemctl restart chrony` (or `chronyc reload sources`) on each host; verify with `chronyc -n sources` that router lines are gone.\n\n### B. Op-router DHCP option 42 removal\n\nOp router currently broadcasts `dhcp_option '42,192.168.13.1'` to LAN DHCP clients. Remove the entry so clients don't get told to use the (non-serving) router as NTP. Mostly future-proofing — Linux chrony already ignores DHCP-supplied NTP unless explicitly opted in, but Windows / other clients may not.\n\n```sh\nuci del dhcp.lan.dhcp_option   # or surgically delete just the 42 entry\nuci commit dhcp\n/etc/init.d/dnsmasq restart\n```\n\n(Verify exact UCI path — the snapshot shows `list dhcp_option '42,192.168.13.1'` so it's a list, not an option; precise uci commands TBD on the device.)\n\n### C. Bridge devices\n\nTracked in [`CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#59`](https://github.com/CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11/issues/59). Will add public-pool fallback (default route via local router + `0.pool.ntp.org` as last resort). Plan to update that issue body in tandem with this one.\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter all three pieces land:\n- `chronyc -n sources` on each Linux host shows **no router IPs** in the source list — only TM2000B + public pools + UNH (salmon).\n- DHCP request from a test client on operator LAN shows no option 42 in the response (`dhclient -v` or `tcpdump`).\n- Bridge devices: see #59 verification.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Mercat**: still unconfigured; needs RDP access and Windows w32tm setup. Separate work.\n- **Restoring router NTP server functionality**: tracked in `project_ntp_rutx11.md` as a deferred follow-on. Currently bridge devices and chrony hosts don't depend on it.\n- **TM2000B `Reach=0` investigation**: all chrony hosts show `^? 192.168.20.123 0 10 0 -` — TM2000B isn't responding to NTP queries today. Could be lost GPS fix, powered off, or service-down. Worth a separate diagnostic pass at the appliance. Not blocking this cleanup.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: #128\n- Bridge: CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#59\n- Memory note: `project_ntp_rutx11.md` (now reflects current reality)\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}